body:
SECOND breakage class, complements mem_19ef48db24d (the ledger-delete / hardcoded-LATEST footgun). When you add migration V(N) that CREATEs a table (or ADDs a column) whose ABSENCE an earlier migration test asserted, that earlier test breaks — and it is NOT a ledger/LATEST issue, so mem_19ef48db24d's checklist does not catch it.

CONCRETE (hit landing V037, #296): migration_034_adds_content_anchored_clone_graph_tables (schema_bootstrap_tests/clones.rs) did apply -> revert clone tables -> truncate_schema_to(33) -> migrate_forward -> assert !conn_table_exists("clone_subblock_postings"). That held only because NO migration <= LATEST created that table. migrate_forward ALWAYS replays every unrecorded migration up to LATEST (there is no "migrate to version K"; it is forward-to-tip), so once V037 adds clone_subblock_postings the post-migrate_forward end state now HAS it -> the absence assert fails. Same trap for a "column absent" assert once a later ALTER adds it.

FIX (keep the deferral assertion, make it future-proof): assert absence against the SPECIFIC migration's DDL in ISOLATION, not the end state of the full ladder. Call that migration's applier on a bare in-memory conn and check the deferred table is absent there:
  let v034_only = rusqlite::Connection::open_in_memory().unwrap();
  crate::index::schema::apply_clone_graph_tables(&v034_only).unwrap();
  assert!(!conn_table_exists(&v034_only, "clone_subblock_postings"));
The apply_* migration fns are pub(crate) (re-exported via `pub(crate) use migrations::*`), reachable from the in-crate #[cfg(test)] tests. This says exactly "V034's DDL does not create X" and survives every future migration that later DOES add X. The NEW test for V(N) then asserts presence after the full apply ladder.

WHY isolation not "drop X again": migrate_forward can't stop before LATEST, so any full-ladder end-state check can only ever see the LATEST shape. Anchor absence to the migration function, not the ladder tip.

(Re-created after a `.rag-rat` index loss; originally mem_19f23364305.)
